#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134901} :activity/id #uuid "fc007430-4cc9-562b-9192-4d3c9dccd500" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135151 :tutorial/content "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+2}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+3}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "cee0a552-60f2-5785-a506-63bd053364c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68428f5-f5bb-5090-b7f9-4971dc931914" :step/math-academy-id 41745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135152 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows but it has two identical rows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can subtract the $1$st row from the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n> *If a square matrix has at least two identical rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "6813d49e-3908-5735-8196-8e1aad5a14e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6889 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Identical Rows or Identical Columns"} :step/id #uuid "e105e6ae-64b8-5812-85e8-b5c3ef7b6c7d" :step/math-academy-id 41746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133466 :question/id #uuid "bb2795c3-6359-50d8-9531-1e9dfae7a2ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-10994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400867}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "f2a27f52-c605-5d01-84c1-49531f59d922" :step/math-academy-id 41747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135153 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows and no identical rows but it has two proportional rows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can add three times the $1$st row to the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n> *If a square matrix has at least two proportional rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe83e8c9-948a-596f-a2d9-c2ae2fe4f335" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6890 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Proportional Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "23e26677-bdeb-5a45-a6d3-4a8d02a72732" :step/math-academy-id 41750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133467 :question/id #uuid "dc780837-b763-5ba9-981a-2373afcb524a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aef330a-81b3-56c4-9559-67df673e8687" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411852}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Two Proportional Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "93111f5e-5fd2-5a5b-81b1-27969a9b5d1d" :step/math-academy-id 41751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135154 :tutorial/content "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\nI. - $A$ has a zero row/column\nII. - $A$ has two identical rows/columns\nIII. - $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\nIV. - $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." :tutorial/id #uuid "9d8b0323-170c-5a60-b372-1a258c4848cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6891 :tutorial/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "4aec093b-050b-5263-9ef2-c875455b8c07" :step/math-academy-id 41754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133468 :question/id #uuid "33ca37e5-c598-52a2-861f-2d5bfa6fd571" :question/math-academy-id "e-10996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351410}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "81571aa7-ce3d-5713-bf67-a49538fee0ef" :step/math-academy-id 41755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135155 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the determinant. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Suppose the columns of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}},$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ a & b & c \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $A$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume $V$ of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $A{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(A)\\right|\n$$\n\n- Now suppose the columns of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}}.$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ p & q \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe $2$-dimensional parallelepiped (i.e., the parallelogram) spanned by vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4af1971404b188df75bb3d62d47ac95340c5e1791066e5358370d743230baa45.png)\n\nThe two-dimensional volume $V$ (i.e., the area) of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(B)\\right|\n$$\n\n- In general, the absolute value of the determinant of an $n\\times n$ matrix $M$ gives the $n$-dimensional volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $M.$\n\nIn this lesson, we studied the following property:\n\n> *$\\det(A) = 0$ if and only if one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns of $A$.*\n\nWhat does this property mean in geometric terms?\n\nThe component $\\det(A) = 0$ implies that the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped formed by the columns of $A$ has zero volume. Conversely, when one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns, it indicates that the corresponding parallelepiped possesses at most $n-1$ distinct dimensions. \n\nConsider the following examples:\n\n- In the case of a $3$-dimensional parallelepiped, if the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\mathbf{c}$ lies within the plane containing $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar. Consequently, we obtain a \"flat\" ($2$-dimensional) figure with a volume of $0$.\n\n- For the parallelogram example, if the vector $\\mathbf{q}$ is a linear combination of the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ (i.e., $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are proportional), then $\\mathbf{q}$ resides on the line containing vector $\\mathbf{p}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are collinear. This results in a $1$-dimensional object with a $2$-dimensional volume (i.e., area) of $0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f7191c6-60ca-5c44-befc-8dbad7779b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6893 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of a Zero Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "8c5dbeea-b2e4-5fe9-9d8c-98750164b70c" :step/math-academy-id 41766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134907}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134042}]}
